-- NEGATIVE float literal regression (plan 0.73 F3). Expected exit code 13
--
-- `-0.5` was a TYPE ERROR until F3 — `inferElabV-RUnaryOp-aux` answered
-- `TypeMismatch Int Float`, because `t-neg`'s premise is at `Int` and `RFloat`
-- infers only at `Float`. It is now ONE literal whose payload is
-- `negate (decimalOf 0 5 1)`, folded by the elaborator exactly as `-5` is
-- (D120): no `arith.neg` is emitted, because there is no float negation to
-- emit — `MArithIR` is Int-only (F4).
--
-- THE PARENTHESES ARE LOAD-BEARING and are a grammar fact, not a typing one.
-- `-` is both prefix and infix, and an application ends at the first token
-- that cannot start an atom — which `-` cannot. So `emitF@T -0.5` would parse
-- as the SUBTRACTION `(emitF@T) - 0.5`.
--
-- Like its positive twin `float-emit-half.once`, the `let _ = … in` shape
-- means the SigOp is never entered at runtime: what this shows is that the
-- literal builds and the process reaches its exit code on all three targets,
-- not what word was passed. The trace-level check on the same literal lives in
-- `FloatEmitSpec` ("Float format in the effect trace"), which sequences with
-- `compose` and reads the emitted word back.
--
-- 0.5 is exact at every supported width, so only the sign bit distinguishes
-- this from the positive twin — which is the narrowest possible statement of
-- what F3 added.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as T

main : IO Unit
main = let _ = emitF@T (-0.5) in exit@S 13
